true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/step4-omp-sonnet-1017-smoke2/omp-38/cells/with/trig-omp-bugfix-regress/1/workspace", "first_after_build": true, "first_side": "with", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/18.1.8 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-cache/bin/omp/1b5c7b53d2c460b2ede60d78591b6826facf0e4d/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`. I checked it by running it, and the existing test in `tests/` passes.\n\n**Cause:** `round(x, 2)` rounds the float's binary value, and `2.675` is stored as `2.67499999\u2026`. Python's `round` also rounds ties to even, not half up as the docstring says.\n\n**Fix:** in `pricing/money.py`, `round_money` now converts the float to a `Decimal` from its shortest printed form (`repr`) and rounds half up with `ROUND_HALF_UP`. It still returns a float.\n\nOther results from the same run:\n- `1.005` gives `1.01`.\n- `0.125` gives `0.13`.\n- `add_vat(10)` gives `12.0`.\n\n**Negatives:** half-up here rounds ties away from zero, so `-2.675` becomes `-2.68`. If you want negative ties rounded toward zero instead, say so.\n\nI didn't add a regression test.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_requests": 0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 1, "replies_ok": 4, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 27656, "requests_cache_write": 85241, "requests_cost_usd": 0.2265881, "requests_count": 4, "requests_input_uncached": 10, "requests_output": 1070, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "omp:trig-omp-bugfix-regress:claude-opus-5-5:trial1", "run_seed": 1016, "sampling_observed": nul